{
  "evidence": "TEXT_D: NHS comprehensive and free; +£540m yearly; restore free eye/dental checks; carers’ support; fairer long‑term care. Strong equality agenda: Human Rights Commission; equal age of consent; repeal Section 28; tackle racism; civil rights for disabled; regionally varied minimum wage; raise personal allowance and a new 50% rate over £100k. Accountable power reforms: Bill of Rights; Freedom of Information; PR for all elections; elected second chamber; fixed terms; 3,000 more police with prevention/restorative justice. Fiscal responsibility claims: “menu with prices,” no promises without prices; competition law and consumer power. Community/loyalty and defence: rural/urban renewal; NATO, retain Trident (with limits). TEXT_E: Care via NHS modernisation, cut waiting lists by 100,000; annual real‑terms funding rises; public health drive; tobacco ad ban; childcare strategy; revive social housing; protect basic pension. Equality through FOI Act; incorporate ECHR; minimum wage via Low Pay Commission; sign EU Social Chapter; ethical foreign policy, move toward 0.7% aid and debt relief; human rights. Authority: tougher sentencing for serious repeat crime; halve time to sentence; more police; zero tolerance; ban all handguns (free vote); end hereditary peers; reformed/independent monetary policy. Loyalty/national unity: ‘for the many, not the few’; Britain leading in Europe and the world; retain Trident, NATO. Proportionality/responsibility: ‘what counts is what works’; golden rule; stick to spending totals; no rise in income‑tax rates; 10p starting rate; windfall levy funding welfare‑to‑work; workplace partnership.",
  "reasoning": "Both texts strongly satisfy Care and Equality, but TEXT_E aligns better with the electorate’s notable Authority and Loyalty priorities through tougher law‑and‑order measures, constitutional accountability, and an explicit emphasis on national unity and leadership. TEXT_E also better fits Proportionality via fiscal rules, “no rise in income‑tax rates,” and welfare‑to‑work, whereas TEXT_D’s stronger redistribution (e.g., a new 50% top rate) tilts more toward equality than proportionality.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
